      "v": 11,
      "zh": "那日，耶路撒冷必有大大的悲哀，如米吉多平原之哈达临门的悲哀。",
      "en": "On that day the lamentation in Jerusalem will be as great as the lamentation at Hadad Rimmon[NOTE:1] in the plain of Megiddo.[NOTE:2]",
      "notes": [
        "“Hadad Rimmon” is a compound of the names of two Canaanite deities, the gods of storm and thunder respectively. The grammar (a subjective genitive) allows, and the problem of comparing Israel’s grief at God’s “wounding” with pagan mourning seems to demand, that this be viewed as a place name, perhaps where Judah lamented the death of good king Josiah (cf. 2 Chr 35:25). However, some translations render this as “for” (NRSV, NCV, TEV, CEV), suggesting a person, while others translate as “of” (KJV, NAB, NASB, NIV, NLT) which is ambiguous.",
